\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

A transformer language model, when processing a token in a context of $n$ prior positions,
distributes attention mass across those positions. Empirically the distribution is
extremely heavy-headed: sorting the attention weights in decreasing order and accumulating,
one finds that a small number of keys already account for almost all of the mass.

The operational summary of this fact is the \emph{knee}. Fix a \emph{gate}
$\tau \in (0,1]$ --- typically $0.98$ --- and define $\kstar$ to be the smallest budget $k$
such that the top $k$ keys retain at least a $\tau$ fraction of the total attention mass
over the context. Sweeping $k$ over a grid and reporting the first pass yields a
\emph{razor bracket}: a largest observed failure and the smallest observed pass.

The measurement motivating this paper is a replication. On a corpus of encyclopedic English
text, the knee is $16$ at context $512$ and $32$ at context $1024$. Repeated verbatim on a
disjoint, independently drawn shard of comparable text, the knee is again $16$ and again
$32$ --- the same integers, not nearby ones --- while random-key control curves agree with
the first run to four decimal places ($0.1775$, $0.3004$). At context $2048$ the two corpora
bracket to $24$--$32$, consistent within the documented grid. Across three contexts, two
corpora and two model sizes the budget remains near thirty keys.

The natural reading is that the knee measures a property of \emph{trained attention} rather
than of the evaluation text. This paper asks what mathematical properties a functional must
have for such a reading to be justified --- and finds that the answer is entirely algebraic.

\subsection{What makes exactness possible}

Two features of the knee conspire to make exact replication achievable where approximate
replication is all one usually gets.

The first is \textbf{linearity}. Written as a statement about the ratio $M_w(k)/M_w(n)$, the
gate is a nonlinear condition on the profile $w$. Written multiplicatively as
$\tau M_w(n) \le M_w(k)$ it is a half space. Both sides of a half space are closed under
addition of profiles and under multiplication by positive scalars, and that two-sided
closure is what allows corpora to be pooled without disturbing a common knee.

The second is \textbf{discreteness}. The knee is an integer-valued minimum. A continuous
perturbation of the retention curve moves the knee only when it carries the curve across the
gate at some grid point. If the reference curve maintains a margin exceeding the
perturbation, the knee cannot move at all.

\section{Corpora, gates, and the knee}

\begin{definition}[Profile and corpus]
An \emph{attention profile} is a function $w : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$, understood as the
attention weights sorted in non-increasing order of importance, with $w_i$ the mass of the
$i$-th key. A profile is a \emph{corpus} if $w_i \ge 0$ for all $i$.
\end{definition}

All results below use only nonnegativity; sortedness is a presentation convention, never a
hypothesis.

\begin{definition}[Head mass, retained fraction]
The \emph{head mass} of $w$ at budget $k$ is $M_w(k) = \sum_{i<k} w_i$. For a context length
$n$ with $M_w(n)>0$, the \emph{retained fraction} at budget $k$ is
$R_w(n,k) = M_w(k)/M_w(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Gate, in linear form]
For a context $n$, budget $k$ and gate $\tau \in \mathbb{R}$, say $w$ \emph{clears}
$(n,k,\tau)$, written $\Cl(w;n,k,\tau)$, if
\[
  \tau\,M_w(n) \;\le\; M_w(k).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Knee]
$\kstar(w;n,\tau) = \min\{\,k \in \mathbb{N} : \Cl(w;n,k,\tau)\,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Consistency of the two forms]\label{lem:consistency}
If $M_w(n)>0$ then $\Cl(w;n,k,\tau) \iff \tau \le R_w(n,k)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Divide by the positive quantity $M_w(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Elementary structure]\label{lem:elem}
For a corpus $w$: $M_w(0)=0$; $M_w(k+1)=M_w(k)+w_k$; $M_w(k) \ge 0$; and $M_w$ is monotone
non-decreasing. Moreover $M_{w+v}(k) = M_w(k)+M_v(k)$ and $M_{cw}(k)=c\,M_w(k)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity in the budget]\label{lem:monobudget}
If $w$ is a corpus and $\Cl(w;n,k,\tau)$ holds, then $\Cl(w;n,l,\tau)$ holds for all
$l \ge k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Existence]\label{lem:exist}
For a corpus $w$ and gate $\tau \le 1$ we have $\Cl(w;n,n,\tau)$; hence the knee exists and
$\kstar(w;n,\tau) \le n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\tau M_w(n) \le M_w(n)$ since $M_w(n)\ge 0$ and $\tau \le 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Galois adjunction]\label{thm:adjunction}
For a corpus $w$ and gate $\tau \le 1$,
$\;\kstar(w;n,\tau) \le k \iff \Cl(w;n,k,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) is the definition of a minimum. ($\Rightarrow$) the minimum is attained by
Lemma~\ref{lem:exist}, and clearing is upward closed by Lemma~\ref{lem:monobudget}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-vacuity]\label{cor:nonvac}
If $k < \kstar(w;n,\tau)$ then $\Cl(w;n,k,\tau)$ fails; conversely a failure at $k$ forces
$k < \kstar$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Razor bracket]\label{thm:bracket}
Let $w$ be a corpus, $\tau \le 1$. If the gate fails at $a$ and holds at $b$, then
$a < \kstar(w;n,\tau) \le b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Pinning]\label{thm:pin}
If the gate holds at $k$ and fails at every $j<k$, then $\kstar(w;n,\tau)=k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Knees are monotone in the context]\label{thm:context}
For a corpus $w$ and $0 \le \tau \le 1$, if $n \le m$ then
$\kstar(w;n,\tau) \le \kstar(w;m,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$M_w(n) \le M_w(m)$, so clearing at context $m$ implies clearing at context $n$ at the same
budget; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:adjunction} to the knee at context $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:context} makes the measured ladder $\kstar(512)=16 \le \kstar(1024)=32$ a
structural fact rather than a fitted trend: no corpus can produce a knee that decreases as
the window grows.
\end{remark}

\section{The corpus cone and the pooling laws}

\begin{definition}[Budget cone]
$\mathcal{C}(n,K,\tau) = \{\, w : \Cl(w;n,K,\tau)\,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cone structure]\label{thm:cone}
$\mathcal{C}(n,K,\tau)$ contains $0$, is closed under addition, and is closed under
multiplication by nonnegative scalars: a convex cone.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from bilinearity of $M$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:elem}): add the two defining inequalities,
or multiply one of them by $c \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The failing side is a cone too]\label{thm:failcone}
If $\Cl(w;n,k,\tau)$ fails and $\Cl(v;n,k,\tau)$ fails, then $\Cl(w+v;n,k,\tau)$ fails.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Add the strict inequalities $M_w(k) < \tau M_w(n)$ and $M_v(k) < \tau M_v(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Filtration]\label{thm:filtration}
For $K \le L$ and $w$ a corpus, $w \in \mathcal{C}(n,K,\tau) \Rightarrow
w \in \mathcal{C}(n,L,\tau)$; the budget cones form an increasing filtration of the corpus
cone whose $K$-th stage is $\{\kstar \le K\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sub-max law]\label{thm:submax}
For corpora $A,B$ and $\tau \le 1$,
$\kstar(A+B;n,\tau) \le \max(\kstar(A;n,\tau), \kstar(B;n,\tau))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $K$ be the maximum. Each of $A,B$ clears at $K$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:monobudget}, hence
$A+B$ clears at $K$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:cone}; conclude by Theorem~\ref{thm:adjunction}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Super-min law]\label{thm:supermin}
For corpora $A,B$ and $\tau \le 1$,
$\min(\kstar(A;n,\tau), \kstar(B;n,\tau)) \le \kstar(A+B;n,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\kstar(A+B)$ were below the minimum, both $A$ and $B$ would fail at that budget
(Corollary~\ref{cor:nonvac}), hence so would $A+B$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:failcone} ---
contradicting that $A+B$ clears at its own knee.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Scale invariance]\label{thm:scale}
For $c>0$, $\kstar(cA;n,\tau) = \kstar(A;n,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact corpus robustness]\label{thm:exact}
If $A,B$ are corpora with $\kstar(A;n,\tau)=\kstar(B;n,\tau)$ and $\tau \le 1$, then
$\kstar(A+B;n,\tau)=\kstar(A;n,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The sandwich of Theorems~\ref{thm:submax} and~\ref{thm:supermin} collapses when
$\min=\max$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Mixing weights are irrelevant]
If $\kstar(A)=\kstar(B)$ and $a,b>0$, then $\kstar(aA+bB)=\kstar(A)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The knee is a non-archimedean valuation]\label{thm:valuation}
On the cone of corpora at fixed $n$ and $\tau \le 1$,
\[
  \kstar(A+B) \le \max\big(\kstar(A),\kstar(B)\big),
  \qquad \kstar(cA)=\kstar(A) \;\; (c>0),
\]
and the sublevel sets of $\kstar$ are the budget cones of Theorem~\ref{thm:filtration}.
\end{theorem}

The formal analogy is with the $p$-adic valuation on $\mathbb{Z}$ or the pole order of a
rational function: an ultrametric, integer-valued, scale-blind functional whose sublevel sets
filter the ambient object. Level sets of such a functional are stable under the ambient
operation --- which is the abstract statement of corpus robustness.

\subsection{Sharpness, and the failure of additivity}

\begin{definition}
On a context of length $2$, let $e_1$ be the profile with $e_1(0)=1$ and $e_1(i)=0$
otherwise, and $e_2$ the profile with $e_2(1)=1$ and $e_2(i)=0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:onehotknees}
For every gate $\tau \in (0,1]$: $\kstar(e_1;2,\tau)=1$ and $\kstar(e_2;2,\tau)=2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Both endpoints are attained]\label{thm:sharp}
$\kstar(e_1+e_2;2,\tfrac12) = 1 = \min(\kstar(e_1),\kstar(e_2))$, while
$\kstar(e_1+e_2;2,\tfrac34) = 2 = \max(\kstar(e_1),\kstar(e_2))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$e_1+e_2$ is uniform with head masses $(0,1,2)$. At gate $\tfrac12$ the condition reads
$1 \le M(k)$, first satisfied at $k=1$; at gate $\tfrac34$ it reads $1.5 \le M(k)$, first
satisfied at $k=2$. Lemma~\ref{lem:onehotknees} supplies the ingredient knees.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The pooled knee is not a function of the ingredient knees]
There exist corpora $A,B$ and gates $\tau \ne \sigma$ with $\kstar(A;\tau)=\kstar(A;\sigma)$,
$\kstar(B;\tau)=\kstar(B;\sigma)$, but $\kstar(A+B;\tau)\ne\kstar(A+B;\sigma)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The tempting formulation of corpus robustness --- additivity, $\kstar(A+B)=\kstar(A)$ ---
is false, and no repair phrased in terms of the ingredient knees alone can succeed. The
correct statement is the sandwich together with its diagonal equality case.
\end{remark}

\section{The four-decimal theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Four-decimal theorem]\label{thm:fourdec}
Let $A,B$ be corpora on a context of length $n$ with $M_A(n)>0$ and $M_B(n)>0$, let
$\tau \le 1$ and $\varepsilon \ge 0$. Suppose
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item ($\varepsilon$-agreement) $|R_A(n,k)-R_B(n,k)| \le \varepsilon$ for every $k \le n$;
  \item (gate margin) $|R_A(n,k)-\tau| > \varepsilon$ for every $k \le n$.
\end{enumerate}
Then $\kstar(A;n,\tau) = \kstar(B;n,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $k \le n$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:consistency}, clearing is equivalent to
$\tau \le R_A(n,k)$, resp.\ $\tau \le R_B(n,k)$. By the margin hypothesis either
$R_A(n,k) > \tau+\varepsilon$, in which case $R_B(n,k) \ge R_A(n,k)-\varepsilon > \tau$ and
both clear; or $R_A(n,k) < \tau-\varepsilon$, in which case
$R_B(n,k) \le R_A(n,k)+\varepsilon < \tau$ and neither clears. So the two gate predicates
coincide on $\{0,\dots,n\}$. Both knees lie in that range by Lemma~\ref{lem:exist}, so $B$
clears at $\kstar(A)$ and $A$ clears at $\kstar(B)$; Theorem~\ref{thm:adjunction} gives both
inequalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bracket transfer]\label{thm:transfer}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:fourdec}, a razor bracket measured on $A$ transfers
verbatim to $B$: a failure at $a$ and a pass at $b$ on $A$ give
$a < \kstar(B;n,\tau) \le b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The measurement instance]
Let $A,B$ be corpora on a context of length $512$ with positive mass, with
$|R_A(512,k)-R_B(512,k)| \le 10^{-4}$ and $|R_A(512,k)-0.98| > 10^{-4}$ for all $k \le 512$.
If the gate $0.98$ fails on $A$ at $k=12$ and holds at $k=16$, then
$\kstar(B;512,0.98) = \kstar(A;512,0.98)$ and $12 < \kstar(B;512,0.98) \le 16$.
\end{corollary}

The reported sweep supplies exactly these inputs: $R_A(512,8)=0.9318$ and
$R_A(512,12)=0.9759$ lie below $0.98$ with margins $0.0482$ and $0.0041$ --- the tighter
margin still forty times the four-decimal tolerance --- and the pass occurs at $k=16$.

\begin{theorem}[Scale-free necessity of the margin]\label{thm:margin}
Let $U$ be the uniform corpus $U_i=1$ on a context of length $2$, at gate $\tfrac12$. Then
$R_U(2,1)=\tfrac12$ exactly, $\kstar(U;2,\tfrac12)=1$, and for \emph{every} tolerance
$\varepsilon>0$ there is a corpus $B$ with $|R_U(2,k)-R_B(2,k)| \le \varepsilon$ for all
$k \le 2$ and $\kstar(B;2,\tfrac12) \ne \kstar(U;2,\tfrac12)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $\delta=\min(1,\varepsilon)>0$ and let $B_0=1-\delta$, $B_i=1$ for $i \ge 1$, so
$M_B=(0,1-\delta,2-\delta)$. The gate reads $1-\tfrac{\delta}{2} \le M_B(k)$, which fails at
$k=1$ and holds at $k=2$, giving $\kstar(B)=2$. For agreement: the retentions match exactly
at $k=0$ and $k=2$, while at $k=1$
\[
  \frac12-\frac{1-\delta}{2-\delta} \;=\; \frac{\delta}{2(2-\delta)} \;\le\;
  \frac{\delta}{2} \;\le\; \varepsilon,
\]
using $2-\delta \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The failure is scale-free: shrinking $\varepsilon$ does not help, because the witness shrinks
with it. No amount of curve agreement forces knee agreement without a gate margin, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:fourdec} is sharp.
\end{remark}

\section{Gate--budget duality and identifiability}

\begin{lemma}[Monotone in the gate]\label{lem:monogate}
For a corpus $w$ and $\tau \le \sigma \le 1$, $\kstar(w;n,\tau) \le \kstar(w;n,\sigma)$.
Hence the sweep $\tau \mapsto \kstar(w;n,\tau)$ is a non-decreasing integer step function.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}[Gate set of a budget]
$G_w(n,k) = \{\tau \le 1 : \kstar(w;n,\tau) \le k\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Duality]\label{thm:duality}
Let $w$ be a corpus with $M_w(n)>0$ and $k \le n$. Then
$G_w(n,k) = (-\infty,\,R_w(n,k)]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$R_w(n,k) \le 1$ since $k \le n$. If $\tau \le 1$ and $\kstar(\tau) \le k$ then
$\Cl(w;n,k,\tau)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:adjunction}, i.e.\ $\tau \le R_w(n,k)$. Conversely
$\tau \le R_w(n,k)$ gives $\tau \le 1$ and $\Cl(w;n,k,\tau)$, hence $\kstar(\tau) \le k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The retention curve is the upper envelope of the sweep]
$R_w(n,k) = \sup G_w(n,k)$ for $k \le n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Identifiability]\label{thm:ident}
Let $A,B$ be corpora on a context of length $n$ with positive mass. If
$\kstar(A;n,\tau)=\kstar(B;n,\tau)$ for \emph{every} gate $\tau \le 1$, then
$R_A(n,k)=R_B(n,k)$ for every $k \le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact step description]
For a corpus $w$ with $M_w(n)>0$, $\tau \le 1$ and $k \le n$,
\[
  \kstar(w;n,\tau)=k \iff \Big(\tau \le R_w(n,k) \text{ and } R_w(n,j)<\tau
  \text{ for all } j<k\Big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:ident} quantifies over the whole gate axis, and that hypothesis is
load-bearing: on a finite grid of gates two corpora can share every measured knee and still
differ in retention strictly between the measured steps. Measuring at more \emph{gates} buys
strictly more information about the corpus; measuring at more \emph{budgets} within a fixed
gate does not, once the razor bracket is closed.
\end{remark}

\section{Domain jumps: a projective law}

\begin{definition}[$\rho$-tilt]
For $\rho>0$, say $B$ is a \emph{$\rho$-tilt} of $A$, written $\Tilt_\rho(A,B)$, if for every
key $i$ one has $B_i \le \rho A_i$ and $A_i \le \rho B_i$.
\end{definition}

This is a ball in the Hilbert projective metric on the corpus cone: the relation is
symmetric, $\Tilt_1(A,A)$ holds for every $A$, and balls of radius $\rho>1$ contain more than
a point (e.g.\ $(\tfrac12,1,1,\dots)$ is a $2$-tilt of the uniform profile).

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:tiltmass}
If $\Tilt_\rho(A,B)$ then $M_B(k) \le \rho M_A(k)$ for every $k$, and symmetrically.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Gate/budget exchange rate]\label{thm:exchange}
Let $\rho>0$, $\tau \ge 0$ and $\Tilt_\rho(A,B)$. If $A$ clears the inflated gate
$\rho^2\tau$ at budget $k$, then $B$ clears the gate $\tau$ at the \emph{same} budget $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Using $M_B(n) \le \rho M_A(n)$ and $M_A(k) \le \rho M_B(k)$,
\[
  \rho\big(\tau M_B(n)\big) \le \rho^2\tau M_A(n) \le M_A(k) \le \rho M_B(k),
\]
and dividing by $\rho>0$ gives $\tau M_B(n) \le M_B(k)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Domain-jump gate window]\label{thm:window}
Let $\rho>0$, $0 \le \tau \le 1$ with $\rho^2\tau \le 1$, and $\Tilt_\rho(A,B)$ with $A,B$
corpora. Then
\[
  \kstar\!\big(A;n,\tau/\rho^2\big) \;\le\; \kstar(B;n,\tau) \;\le\;
  \kstar\!\big(A;n,\rho^2\tau\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Exactness at $\rho=1$]
If $\Tilt_1(A,B)$ then $\kstar(B;n,\tau)=\kstar(A;n,\tau)$ for all $0 \le \tau \le 1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Deployment criterion: a flat window]\label{thm:flat}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:window}, if the reference sweep is flat across the
window, $\kstar(A;n,\tau/\rho^2)=\kstar(A;n,\rho^2\tau)$, then every $\rho$-tilted corpus ---
including one from an unmeasured domain --- has exactly that knee.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The sharp coordinate: missing mass}

Theorem~\ref{thm:window} inflates the gate by $\rho^2$, which is vacuous at $\tau=0.98$
unless $\rho \lesssim 1.01$. The repair is to work in the complementary coordinate.

\begin{lemma}[Tail representation and tilt inflation]\label{lem:tail}
For $k \le n$, $M_w(n)-M_w(k)=\sum_{k \le i<n} w_i$; and if $\Tilt_\rho(A,B)$ then
$M_B(n)-M_B(k) \le \rho\,(M_A(n)-M_A(k))$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp domain-jump law]\label{thm:sharpjump}
Let $\rho>0$, $\delta \ge 0$, $A$ a corpus with $\Tilt_\rho(A,B)$ and $k \le n$. If $A$
clears the gate $1-\delta$ at budget $k$, then $B$ clears the gate $1-\rho^2\delta$ at the
same budget $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $\Cl(A;n,k,1-\delta)$ we get $M_A(n)-M_A(k) \le \delta M_A(n)$. Lemma~\ref{lem:tail}
bounds $B$'s tail by $\rho\delta M_A(n)$, and $M_A(n) \le \rho M_B(n)$ upgrades this to
$\rho^2\delta M_B(n)$; rearranging gives $(1-\rho^2\delta)M_B(n) \le M_B(k)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\kstar(B;n,1-\rho^2\delta) \le \kstar(A;n,1-\delta)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
At $\tau=0.98$ the missing mass is $\delta=0.02$; a distortion $\rho=1.1$ inflates it to
$0.0242$, i.e.\ an effective gate of $0.9758$ --- comfortably inside a measured sweep. In the
retained coordinate the same distortion would demand a gate of $1.19$, which is meaningless.
\end{remark}

\subsection{An explicit budget under a geometric tail}

\begin{definition}[Geometric tail]
A corpus $A$ has an \emph{$r$-geometric retention tail} at context $n$ if
$1-r^k \le R_A(n,k)$ for all $k \le n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Domain-jump budget bound]\label{thm:budget}
Let $A$ have an $r$-geometric tail at context $n$ with $M_A(n)>0$, let $\Tilt_\rho(A,B)$ with
$\rho>0$, and let $\tau \ge 0$. Then for any budget $K \le n$ with $r^K \le 1-\rho^2\tau$ we
have $\kstar(B;n,\tau) \le K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Solving for the budget]
The condition holds for
$K = \big\lceil \log(1/(1-\rho^2\tau)) / \log(1/r) \big\rceil$, so the cost of a domain jump
of projective radius $\rho$ is $O\big(\log(1/(1-\rho^2\tau))/\log(1/r)\big)$ keys ---
logarithmic in the distortion, with no re-measurement on the new domain.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
No bound on $\kstar(B;n,\tau)$ in terms of $\kstar(A;n,\tau)$ alone can hold uniformly over
tilt balls: the uniform corpus is a bounded tilt of a geometric one only for large $\rho$,
and its knee grows linearly in the context while the geometric corpus's knee stays
logarithmic. The gate coordinate is the only one in which a tilt acts boundedly.
\end{remark}

\section{The knee fan}

\begin{definition}[Knee cell]
For fixed $n$ and $\tau$, $\mathcal{K}(n,\tau,K) = \{\,w : w \text{ a corpus},\;
\kstar(w;n,\tau)=K\,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Two measurements cut out a cell]\label{thm:cell}
For $\tau \le 1$ and $K \ge 1$,
\[
  w \in \mathcal{K}(n,\tau,K) \iff w \text{ is a corpus},\; \Cl(w;n,K,\tau),\;
  \text{and } \neg\,\Cl(w;n,K-1,\tau).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Polyhedrality]
Each cell is the intersection of the corpus cone with one closed and one open half space.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Cells are cones]
For $\tau \le 1$, each cell is closed under positive scaling and under addition; hence for
$w,v \in \mathcal{K}(n,\tau,K)$ and $a,b>0$ one has $aw+bv \in \mathcal{K}(n,\tau,K)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Partition]
Cells with distinct labels are disjoint, and every corpus lies in the cell of some label
$K \le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness of the fan]\label{thm:complete}
Let $0<\tau \le 1$. For every $K$ with $1 \le K \le n$, the one-hot corpus $\delta_{K-1}$ has
$\kstar(\delta_{K-1};n,\tau)=K$; the zero profile realises label $0$. Hence every cell is
non-empty and $\kstar$ maps the corpus cone onto $\{0,1,\dots,n\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Structure theorem]\label{thm:structure}
For $0<\tau \le 1$ the cone of corpora at context $n$ is the disjoint union of the knee cells
$\mathcal{K}(n,\tau,K)$, $K=0,\dots,n$; each cell is non-empty, convex, closed under positive
combinations, and cut out by exactly two linear measurements --- a pass at $K$ and a failure
at $K-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Corpus robustness is \emph{membership in a cell}, and a razor bracket reporting a failure at
$K-1$ and a pass at $K$ \emph{is} a cell certificate; nothing weaker will do, since knowing
only that the gate clears at $K$ places the corpus in a union of cells. Conversely, because
every cell is non-empty, no argument from attention geometry alone can force a particular
knee: the measurement is doing irreducible work.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms and deployment}

\subsection{Knee sweep by bisection}
Clearing is monotone in the budget, so the predicate $k \mapsto \Cl(w;n,k,\tau)$ is a step
from false to true and the knee is located by binary search. With prefix sums precomputed in
$O(n)$ time, each gate query costs $O(\log n)$ and a sweep over $G$ gates costs
$O(n + G\log n)$. The bracket returned is exactly the cell certificate of
Theorem~\ref{thm:cell}.

\subsection{Margin-certified replication}
Given two measured retention curves and a gate, compute
$\varepsilon = \max_{k \le n}|R_A(k)-R_B(k)|$ and $m = \min_{k \le n}|R_A(k)-\tau|$ in $O(n)$
time. If $m>\varepsilon$, the two knees are provably equal and the pair $(\varepsilon,m)$ is
the certificate. This converts an observation into a proof and is the recommended report
format for a cross-corpus replication.

\subsection{Cross-domain budget transfer}
Given a reference corpus $A$, a projective radius $\rho$, and a gate $\tau=1-\delta$, compute
$A$'s knee at the tail-inflated gate $1-\rho^2\delta$; Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpjump} certifies
that every $\rho$-tilt of $A$ is served by that budget. If in addition $A$'s sweep is flat
across the window, Theorem~\ref{thm:flat} upgrades the bound to an exact prediction. Under a
fitted geometric tail with ratio $r$, Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} gives the budget in closed
form.

\subsection{Reading of the measurement}
The measured knees $16$ at context $512$ and $32$ at context $1024$ obey the forced
monotonicity of Theorem~\ref{thm:context}. The exact cross-corpus agreement is certified by
Theorem~\ref{thm:fourdec} with $\varepsilon=10^{-4}$ and observed margins of order
$10^{-3}$--$10^{-2}$. The stability of the budget near thirty keys is consistent with a
geometric tail: fitting $r$ so that $r^{32} \approx 0.02$ gives $r \approx 0.885$, and the
budget bound then predicts single-digit growth under moderate domain distortion. Finally,
Theorem~\ref{thm:structure} states the honest epistemic position: the observation places the
trained model's attention profile in a particular cell of the fan, every cell is inhabited,
and the value $32$ is therefore a fact about this model, established by measurement.

\section{Limitations}

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item The theory is about profiles, not about training: it explains what follows once
        attention is heavy-headed, not why it is.
  \item Identifiability needs all gates; a finite sweep does not determine the retention
        curve between measured steps.
  \item The tilt radius must be estimated, which requires some measurement on the new domain,
        albeit far less than a full knee sweep.
  \item Sortedness is a presentation convention; all theorems use only nonnegativity.
  \item The motivating replication covers two corpora from the same textual family, three
        context lengths and two model sizes; a genuine domain jump remains to be measured.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Future directions}

\subsection{Per-layer knee fans and a tensor decomposition of the budget}
Each attention layer carries its own corpus cone and its own knee fan. We conjecture the
model-level cell is the \emph{product} cell: the model knee at gate $\tau$ equals the maximum
of the per-layer knees at gates redistributed by the layers' mass shares, with level sets
remaining polyhedral in the joint profile. Since pooling layers is a direct sum of corpora
and the knee is a non-archimedean valuation,
$\kstar\big(\bigoplus_l w_l\big) \le \max_l \kstar(w_l)$ holds verbatim; the open part is the
matching lower bound and the exact gate redistribution.

\subsection{A Hilbert-metric Lipschitz bound for the knee sweep}
Let $d(A,B)$ be the least $\log\rho$ with $\Tilt_\rho(A,B)$. We conjecture a uniform
Lipschitz bound in the missing-mass coordinate,
\[
  1-R_B(n,k) \;\le\; e^{2d(A,B)}\big(1-R_A(n,k)\big) \qquad \text{for all } k,
\]
with equality attained on extreme rays of the cone. Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpjump} is the
pointwise instance; the conjecture asks for it uniformly in $k$.

\subsection{Further directions}
Measure domain-jump corpora (code, mathematics, non-English text) and estimate the tilt
radius directly, testing the flat-window criterion against observation; close the gap between
the oracle budget studied here and a policy budget realisable by a cheap online selector;
replace the single model-level number by a vector of per-layer knees; extend the ladder to
larger parameter counts and to quantized-offload regimes; and determine which of the results
survive when the retention criterion is not linear.

\section{Conclusion}

Writing the retention gate multiplicatively, $\tau M_w(n) \le M_w(k)$, so that it becomes a
half space in the cone of nonnegative attention profiles, reduces the empirical stability of
the attention knee to algebra. The knee is a scale-invariant, integer-valued,
non-archimedean valuation on that cone; the pooling sandwich is sharp at both ends and the
knee is not additive; four-decimal agreement of retention curves forces exact agreement of
knees provided a gate margin exceeds the tolerance, and at zero margin no tolerance suffices;
the gate sweep and the retention curve are Galois-dual, so a published budget table is a
complete invariant of a corpus's measurable content; domain jumps act projectively and
inflate missing mass by $\rho^2$, costing only logarithmically many extra keys under a
geometric tail; and the corpus cone is a fan of non-empty polyhedral cells, each certified by
precisely the two measurements a razor bracket reports.

The measured thirty-key budget is thus best understood not as a fitted constant but as a
\emph{cell label} --- a discrete invariant that replicates exactly because the geometry
around it is linear, the invariant is discrete, and the measurement has margin.

